 The engine flags behind it: `--layer-split K1[,K2..]|auto` and `--split-device D1[,D2..]` (the later stages'
 devices; default the next visible ones). `--layer-split K --split-device 0` runs both stages on one card sharing
 everything - the bit-exact check of the hand-off, not a speed mode.
+
+An explicit split can return to the first GPU for its final stage. For example, with the 5070 Ti visible as
+CUDA0 and the 4060 as CUDA1, `--layer-split 43,47 --split-device 1,0` runs layers 0–42 on the 5070 Ti,
+43–46 on the 4060, then layer 47, the output head and MTP on the 5070 Ti. In a server config, use
+`"gpu": [0, 1]`, `"layer_split": "43,47"`, and add `"--split-device", "1,0"` to `args`.
+The returning stage keeps its own weights, session, expert cache and prompt buffers. Its cache and remaining
+allocations are reserved before the first stage fills its cache on that GPU. This path has two hand-offs per
+verification window; it is not searched by `auto`. Other later stages must still use distinct GPUs.
+
+To leave all final transformer layers on CUDA1 while running the head and MTP on CUDA0, use an explicit
+boundary and `--head-device 0`. For example, `--layer-split 44 --head-device 0` runs layers 0–43 on CUDA0,
+44–47 on CUDA1, and the output head/MTP on CUDA0. A config uses `"gpu": [0, 1]`, `"layer_split": 44`,
+and adds `"--head-device", "0"` to `args`. The engine appends a head-only stage that receives the final
+residual and owns its head/MTP buffers; it has no transformer experts. This option requires an explicit
+multi-GPU split. The available expert cache must still be verified for the chosen boundary.
+
+Measured 2026-10-04 on an i5-13500, 64 GB RAM, RTX 5070 Ti 16 GB + RTX 4060 8 GB with IQ4_XS,
+Q2 MTP (full 248320-token draft vocabulary), int8 KV, 131072 context capacity, prefill 512 and a 1248 MiB reserve:
+layers 44–47 held all 2048 experts on the 4060 (5.22 GiB); layers 43–47 held only 2116 of 2560.
+The saved marketing/Python tasks decoded at 38.66/39.30 tok/s. The identical initial 24796-token Python prompt
+read at 248.0 tok/s. These are single-run results; answer lengths and histories differ between placements.
+[Full comparison, settings and validation](../evaluations/2026-10-04-head-on-primary/REPORT.md).
 
 **auto** tries every placement for two or three cards; beyond that it shares layers in proportion to estimated GPU
 speed. It estimates layer compute time and the cost of experts missing from the caches, with hotter pairs weighted
